@Subroutines
.
=SUBROUTINES
.
.SUB name(parameters) ... ENDSUB. ENDSUB can be written END SUB. A SUB is
.called by its name and its brackets. CALL in front is optional. The brackets
.are not: they make the name a call, even with no arguments.
.
:SUB SHOW(N)
:  PRINT N
:ENDSUB
:SHOW(7)
:CALL SHOW(7)
.
.A SUB with a value has a type, by its mark or by AS. RETURN gives the value,
.and the SUB is used in an expression. A SUB name with no mark and no AS has
.no value, so a SUB that returns a BYTE is written SUB F() AS BYTE.
.
:SUB MAX%(A%, B%)
:  IF A% > B% THEN RETURN A%
:  RETURN B%
:ENDSUB
:SUB HALF(N AS WORD) AS WORD
:  RETURN N / 2
:ENDSUB
:DIM T%
:T% = MAX%(3, HALF(100))
.
.A SUB that returns a STRING has the $ mark or AS STRING: SUB FIRST3$(S$) or
.SUB WRAP(T AS STRING) AS STRING. Its value is assigned, printed, compared
.and joined with + as any string is.
.
:DIM A$ * 20
:SUB FIRST3$(S$)
:  RETURN MID$(S$, 1, 3)
:ENDSUB
:A$ = FIRST3$("HELLO")
:PRINT FIRST3$("WORLD") + "!"
.
.  - A parameter is a BYTE, a WORD or a STRING, by its mark or by AS. With
.    neither it is a BYTE: SUB SHOW(N). An argument is worked into its
.    parameter's type: a WORD into a BYTE parameter keeps its low byte. A
.    STRING parameter has no size, and the SUB works on the caller's string.
.    See Strings.
.  - The number of arguments must match the parameters.
.  - RETURN leaves the SUB at once. In a SUB with no value, RETURN has
.    nothing after it.
.  - A SUB with a value needs at least one RETURN with a value. Its end, when
.    the body reaches it, gives 0, or "" in a STRING SUB.
.  - A STRING result is at most 255 characters. An assignment cuts it to the
.    size of the destination.
.  - A STRING result passed to a SUB is passed as a copy.
.  - A SUB may be called before it is written.
.  - A SUB with a value may be called as a statement. The value is lost.
.  - The main program jumps over a SUB, so a SUB can sit anywhere outside a
.    block.
.  - A SUB cannot call itself. A SUB that calls itself stops the compile with
.    A SUB CANNOT CALL ITSELF.
.  - SUBs cannot call each other. A loop of calls between SUBs, A calls B and
.    B calls A or a longer loop, stops the compile with SUBS CALL EACH OTHER
.    at the line of one of the calls.
.
=Locals
.
.Parameters, and names DIMed in the SUB, are the SUB's locals. A local with
.the same name as a main program variable hides it inside the SUB. Main
.program variables DIMed before the SUB can be used in it. Labels belong to
.the SUB too, so no GOTO crosses into or out of one.
.
.  - A local does not keep its value between calls.
.  - A BYTE or WORD local, and a local array, starts undefined. Set it before
.    you read it.
.  - A local string is set empty each time its DIM line runs.
.  - Only the SUB itself can name its locals. Inside SHOW, SHOW.N is N.
.    Outside SHOW, PRINT SHOW.N stops the compile with LOCAL TO A SUB:
.    SHOW.N.
.  - The locals of one SUB take at most 16384 bytes. The DIM that passes it
.    stops the compile with LOCALS TOO BIG.
.  - The SUBs of one level of the call graph share the memory of their
.    locals. The compiler does not see a call through a pointer or SYS. A SUB
.    called that way may share that memory with a SUB that is running.
.  - The main program's variables are not locals. Each has its own address
.    and keeps its value.
.
